      "content": "It's understandable to balance the need for thoroughness with the urgency of your filing deadline. For internal records, it might be wise to document these findings as preliminary. However, in committing to such a position, I must advise that it's critical to heed the words of \"proactive.\" Regulatory agencies often have protocols in place not just for dealing with known issues but also for managing unforeseen ones that emerge. Ignoring minor findings despite having internal data on them could indeed lead to regulatory inquiries that might cancel our advantageous filing timeline. \n\nEven if the increased arrhythmic events represent a small subset and are self-limiting, their documentation and subsequent monitoring will be much smoother if addressed head-on. Consequently, I suggest preparing a detailed internal note, underlining that these findings stem from a very specific demographic and circumstances. Further comprehensive tests, including a longer follow-up period, should be included in non-filing documentation as part of our roadmap to ensure full transparency. Adhering to the best practices of thorough reporting now could fortify CardioFlow's reputation for safety prior to filing, thus potentially propelling us through the competitive rush.",
